  "performance_limits": "3 managed and15 PTY hosted debug samples per OS qualify existing runtime scenarios; no statistical baseline or worktree creation throughput/latency benchmark.",
  "complexity": "Pure request validation is linear in bounded path/ref/OID input. Verification is one bounded-result Git invocation; backend capture contract remains separate. Actual creation/checkouts and trusted Git ancillary work scale with repository/checkout contents; existing managed output retention bounds stored output, not Git memory or external effects. No competing receipt registry or scheduler is introduced.",
  "remaining_limits": [
    "Provider qualification is Local/native on Linux and macOS. No live SSH/Boat or Windows creation qualification; custom IBackend ownership semantics are not inferred from local tests.",
    "Every start_create call re-verifies the commit before reaching the managed creation claim, so unavailable Git/repository/objects can block that retry. Existing Workspace.run(savedRunId) permits direct inspection/reconnection without verification. No newRunId is safe merely because delivery is unknown.",
    "Verification and later execution are not an atomic repository binding; caller must control repository/object namespace and competing destination writers. Git may accept an existing empty directory and can clean up in-progress paths or leave a branch/partial state after failure.",
    "Host Git hooks, filters, promisor fetching and configured diagnostics remain trusted behavior. Verification happens before the creation claim and can repeat ancillary I/O; arbitrary external effects are not exactly-once.",
    "No SDK worktree removal, automatic provider attachment, resource ownership inferred from inventory paths, Git-aware checkpoint reconstruction, coordinator, deployment or remote billable resource is added."
  ],
